pages.content:
CL SDK provides a modular prompt system for building insurance-aware conversational agents. Prompts are composed from independent modules that adapt to the platform and communication intent.

## Quick start

```typescript

const ctx: AgentContext = {
  platform: "email",
  intent: "direct",
  siteUrl: "https://app.example.com",
  companyName: "Acme Insurance",
  userName: "Jane Smith",
};

const systemPrompt = buildAgentSystemPrompt(ctx);
// Use with any LLM: pass as the system message
```

## How it works

`buildAgentSystemPrompt(ctx)` composes all modules in order, filtering out any that return `null`:

```
Identity          → Who the agent is, company context
Company context   → Organization-specific information (if provided)
Intent            → Behavior rules for direct / mediated / observed
Formatting        → Platform-specific output rules
Safety            → Scope guardrails, anti-hallucination, prompt injection defense
Coverage gaps     → Gap detection guidance (null in some intents)
COI routing       → Certificate of insurance handling
Quotes/policies   → How to differentiate document types
Memory            → Cross-conversation continuity guidance
```

## AgentContext

The context object drives all prompt composition:

```typescript
interface AgentContext {
  platform: Platform;           // "email" | "chat" | "sms" | "slack" | "discord"
  intent: CommunicationIntent;  // "direct" | "mediated" | "observed"
  platformConfig?: PlatformConfig;
  companyName?: string;
  companyContext?: string;       // Organization-specific context string
  siteUrl: string;
  userName?: string;
  coiHandling?: "broker" | "user" | "member" | "ignore";
  brokerName?: string;
  brokerContactName?: string;
  brokerContactEmail?: string;
  agentName?: string;           // Display name (defaults to "CL-0 Agent")
  linkGuidance?: string;        // Custom link formatting guidance
}
```

## Individual modules

Each module is independently importable for custom prompt composition:

```typescript
  buildIdentityPrompt,
  buildSafetyPrompt,
  buildFormattingPrompt,
  buildCoverageGapPrompt,
  buildCoiRoutingPrompt,
  buildQuotesPoliciesPrompt,
  buildConversationMemoryGuidance,
  buildIntentPrompt,
} from "@claritylabs/cl-sdk";
```

### Custom composition

Build your own prompt from a subset of modules:

```typescript
const customPrompt = [
  buildIdentityPrompt(ctx),
  buildSafetyPrompt(ctx),
  buildFormattingPrompt(ctx),
  // Skip coverage gaps, COI routing, etc.
].filter(Boolean).join("\n\n");
```

## Document context

Build ranked document context for agent responses:

```typescript

const { context, relevantPolicyIds, relevantQuoteIds } =
  buildDocumentContext(policies, quotes, "What's my GL limit?");
// Returns policies/quotes ranked by relevance to the query
```

## Conversation memory

Add conversation history context:

```typescript

const memoryContext = buildConversationMemoryContext(previousConversations);
```

## Intent classification

Classify incoming messages before routing to the agent:

```typescript

const classificationPrompt = buildClassifyMessagePrompt("email");
// Returns a prompt that classifies whether a message is insurance-related
// and suggests an intent
```

CL SDK exports Claude `tool_use`-compatible tool schemas for common insurance agent operations. These are **schema-only** — CL SDK provides the definitions, and consumers implement the execution logic.

## Available tools

### Document Lookup

Search and retrieve insurance documents by ID, policy number, carrier, or free-text query.

```typescript
```

**Input schema:**

| Parameter | Type | Required | Description |
|-----------|------|----------|-------------|
| `id` | `string` | No | Exact document ID |
| `query` | `string` | No | Free-text search query |
| `documentType` | `"policy" \| "quote"` | No | Filter by document type |

### COI Generation

Request generation of a Certificate of Insurance for a specific policy.

```typescript
```

**Input schema:**

| Parameter | Type | Required | Description |
|-----------|------|----------|-------------|
| `policyId` | `string` | Yes | Policy ID to generate COI for |
| `holderName` | `string` | Yes | Certificate holder name |
| `holderAddress` | `string` | No | Certificate holder address |
| `additionalInsured` | `boolean` | No | Add holder as additional insured |

### Coverage Comparison

Compare coverages across two or more insurance documents.

```typescript
```

**Input schema:**

| Parameter | Type | Required | Description |
|-----------|------|----------|-------------|
| `documentIds` | `string[]` | Yes | Document IDs to compare |
| `policyTypes` | `string[]` | No | Filter to specific policy types |

## Using all tools

Import all tools as an array:

```typescript

// AGENT_TOOLS = [DOCUMENT_LOOKUP_TOOL, COI_GENERATION_TOOL, COVERAGE_COMPARISON_TOOL]
```

## Integration example

```typescript

const systemPrompt = buildAgentSystemPrompt(ctx);

const { text, toolCalls } = await generateText({
  model: yourModel,
  system: systemPrompt,
  tools: Object.fromEntries(
    AGENT_TOOLS.map(tool => [tool.name, {
      description: tool.description,
      parameters: tool.input_schema,
    }])
  ),
  messages: conversationHistory,
});

// Handle tool calls with your own implementations
for (const call of toolCalls) {
  switch (call.toolName) {
    case "document_lookup":
      // Query your database
      break;
    case "coi_generation":
      // Generate COI PDF
      break;
    case "coverage_comparison":
      // Build comparison table
      break;
  }
}
```

## ToolDefinition type

```typescript

interface ToolDefinition {
  name: string;
  description: string;
  input_schema: {
    type: "object";
    properties: Record<string, unknown>;
    required?: string[];
  };
}
```

## Pipeline functions

### `createExtractor(config)`

Create a source-tree extraction pipeline. For PDF inputs, callers must provide preprocessed `SourceSpan[]` through `ExtractOptions.sourceSpans`. For Docling inputs, CL SDK derives spans from the serialized Docling document. If extraction receives no spans, it throws:

```text
cl-sdk extraction now requires preprocessed source spans. Run LiteParse or another source-span preprocessor and pass ExtractOptions.sourceSpans; legacy raw-PDF page-map extraction has been removed.
```

| Parameter | Type | Description |
|-----------|------|-------------|
| `config` | `ExtractorConfig` | Pipeline configuration |

**Returns:** `{ extract(input: ExtractionInput, documentId?: string, options?: ExtractOptions): Promise<ExtractionResult> }`

```typescript

const extractor = createExtractor({
  generateObject: myGenerateObject,
  onProgress: (msg) => console.log(msg),
  onTokenUsage: (usage) => console.log(`Tokens: ${usage.inputTokens} in, ${usage.outputTokens} out`),
});

const sourceSpans = buildPageSourceSpans([
  { documentId: "policy-123", sourceKind: "policy_pdf", pageNumber: 1, text: pageOneText },
]);

const result = await extractor.extract(pdfBase64, "policy-123", { sourceSpans });
```

`ExtractionInput` is either a `PdfInput` value (`string`, `URL`, `Uint8Array`, or `{ fileId }`) plus `ExtractOptions.sourceSpans`, or a Docling input:

```typescript
const result = await extractor.extract({
  kind: "docling_document",
  document: doclingDocumentJson,
  sourceKind: "policy_pdf",
}, "policy-123");
```

Docling conversion is host-owned. CL SDK consumes serialized `DoclingDocument` JSON, normalizes page text and provenance into source spans, and then runs the same source-tree extraction path.

The current extraction path is:

1. **Normalize source spans** -- merge Docling-derived spans and caller-provided spans, normalize text, remove duplicate or unusable spans, and derive `sourceChunks`.
2. **Persist source evidence** -- when `sourceStore` is configured, save source spans and chunks before extraction.
3. **Build the source tree** -- deterministically construct document, page group, page, form, endorsement, section, schedule, clause, table, row, cell, and text nodes from existing spans.
4. **Apply semantic grouping** -- group declarations, policy forms, endorsements, sections, and schedules from parser titles and source text. The grouping code may relabel or regroup existing source nodes; it does not invent source text, source span IDs, pages, or bounding boxes.
5. **Extract the operational profile** -- call `generateObject` with a bounded source-evidence packet for product-critical facts such as policy number, named insured, insurer, broker, policy period, coverage units, limits, deductibles, premiums, and endorsement support.
6. **Validate and merge citations** -- reject uncited facts and source IDs that are not present in the current source tree/spans.
7. **Clean up coverages** -- when coverage units exist, run one bounded source-backed cleanup pass that keeps, drops, or updates existing coverage rows by `coverageIndex`.
8. **Project compatibility output** -- materialize `document`, `documentMetadata`, and `documentOutline` as views over `sourceTree` and `operationalProfile`; return `chunks: []` on this v3 path.

<Callout type="info">
  The `extract` method accepts an optional `documentId`. If omitted, a timestamped ID is generated automatically.
</Callout>

---

### `chunkDocument(doc)`

Convert an `InsuranceDocument` into `DocumentChunk[]`. This remains available for legacy or manually assembled documents, but source-tree extraction returns `chunks: []` because broad structured chunks are no longer the primary evidence corpus.

```typescript

const chunks = chunkDocument(document);
await memoryStore.addChunks(chunks);
```

For newly extracted v3 documents, index `result.sourceTree` for retrieval and keep `result.sourceSpans` for exact highlights.

---

## Configuration types

### `ExtractorConfig`

```typescript
interface ExtractorConfig {
  /** Provider callback for structured output with Zod schemas */
  generateObject: GenerateObject;
  /** Called after each model call with token counts, when the provider reports usage */
  onTokenUsage?: (usage: TokenUsage) => void;
  /** Called with human-readable progress messages during extraction */
  onProgress?: (message: string) => void;
  /** Optional async logger */
  log?: LogFn;
  /** Pass-through provider options forwarded to extraction model calls */
  providerOptions?: Record<string, unknown>;
  /** Optional source evidence persistence */
  sourceStore?: SourceStore;
  /** Optional quality gate behavior */
  qualityGate?: "off" | "warn" | "strict";
  /** Optional model metadata for resolving default task budgets */
  modelCapabilities?: ModelCapabilities;
  /** Optional model metadata by task kind for routed hosts */
  modelCapabilitiesByTaskKind?: Partial<Record<ModelTaskKind, ModelCapabilities>>;
  /** Optional per-task budget constraints */
  modelBudgetConstraints?: Partial<Record<ModelTaskKind, ModelBudgetConstraint>>;
}
```

`generateText`, `convertPdfToImages`, page-map concurrency, focused extractor concurrency, review mode, and checkpoint options are not part of the current extraction config. They may still exist in other SDK workflows or older release notes, but they are not used by `createExtractor()` in the published 3.2.13 API.

### `ExtractionInput`

```typescript
type ExtractionInput =
  | PdfInput
  | {
      kind: "docling_document";
      document: DoclingDocumentLike;
      sourceKind?: SourceKind;
    };
```

`PdfInput` still identifies the source document for compatibility, but policy extraction does not send raw PDF bytes or page images as the primary extraction context. Pass source spans produced by LiteParse, Docling, PDF.js, OCR, or another parser.

### `ExtractOptions`

```typescript
interface ExtractOptions {
  /** Caller-provided raw source spans for this document. */
  sourceSpans?: SourceSpan[];
}
```

For PDF inputs, `sourceSpans` are required. For Docling inputs, they are optional and are merged with the spans derived from the Docling document.

### `ExtractionResult`

```typescript
interface ExtractionResult {
  /** Compatibility policy or quote projection */
  document: InsuranceDocument;
  /** Empty on v3 source-tree extraction paths */
  chunks: DocumentChunk[];
  /** Source spans used for grounding */
  sourceSpans: SourceSpan[];
  /** Source chunks derived from sourceSpans */
  sourceChunks: SourceChunk[];
  /** Canonical source hierarchy */
  sourceTree?: DocumentSourceNode[];
  /** Source-backed product fact projection */
  operationalProfile?: PolicyOperationalProfile;
  /** Non-blocking extraction warnings */
  warnings?: string[];
  /** Aggregate token usage across model calls that reported usage */
  tokenUsage: TokenUsage;
  /** Whether provider callbacks returned usage for each model call */
  usageReporting: {
    modelCalls: number;
    callsWithUsage: number;
    callsMissingUsage: number;
  };
  /** Timing and model-call performance summary */
  performanceReport: PerformanceReport;
  /** Source-tree warning and artifact report */
  reviewReport: ExtractionReviewReport;
}
```

`ExtractionResult.document` is a compatibility projection. Product surfaces that need source truth should prefer `sourceTree` for wording/hierarchy and `operationalProfile` for product-critical facts.

### `PolicyOperationalProfile`

The operational profile is the bounded source-backed fact layer:

```typescript
interface PolicyOperationalProfile {
  documentType: "policy" | "quote";
  linesOfBusiness: AcordLobCode[];
  policyNumber?: SourceBackedValue;
  namedInsured?: SourceBackedValue;
  insurer?: SourceBackedValue;
  broker?: SourceBackedValue;
  effectiveDate?: SourceBackedValue;
  expirationDate?: SourceBackedValue;
  retroactiveDate?: SourceBackedValue;
  premium?: SourceBackedValue;
  operationsDescription?: SourceBackedValue;
  declarationFacts: OperationalDeclarationFact[];
  coverages: OperationalCoverageLine[];
  parties: OperationalParty[];
  endorsementSupport: OperationalEndorsementSupport[];
  sourceNodeIds: string[];
  sourceSpanIds: string[];
  warnings: string[];
}
```

`linesOfBusiness` is the single top-level policy classification field. Values are ACORD line of business codes. Serialized legacy `policyTypes` blobs are accepted at parse time and normalized to `linesOfBusiness`.

`declarationFacts` is the source-backed declaration fact stream for host persistence. It carries named-insured identity details such as mailing address, DBA, entity type, tax ID, additional named insured rows, and other key declarations with normalized values plus source IDs.

`parties` keeps policy-scoped named-insured, producer/broker, insurer/carrier, and MGA/administrator identities. Each party may carry a partial or complete `OperationalAddress`; the compatibility document projection materializes only complete named-insured, producer, and insurer addresses. `operationsDescription` is the insured's directly source-backed business or operations description rather than an inference from coverages or external research.

---

## Schema utilities

### `toStrictSchema(schema)`

Transform a Zod schema for strict structured output compatibility. Recursively converts `.optional()` properties to `.nullable()` so all properties appear in the JSON Schema `required` array.

```typescript

const strict = toStrictSchema(z.object({
  name: z.string(),
  age: z.number().optional(),
}));
```

<Callout type="info">
  You do not need to call this yourself for `createExtractor()`; the pipeline applies it automatically before every `generateObject` call.
</Callout>

### `safeGenerateObject(generateObject, params, options?)`

Wraps a `generateObject` call with schema strictification, retry on transient errors, and optional fallback values.

```typescript

const { object } = await safeGenerateObject(
  myGenerateObject,
  { prompt: "...", schema: MySchema, maxTokens: 1024 },
  {
    fallback: { status: "unknown" },
    maxRetries: 2,
  },
);
```

## Pipeline functions

### `createQueryAgent(config)`

Create a query agent that answers questions against stored documents with citation-backed provenance.
It also supports per-query multimodal attachments such as photos, PDFs, and raw text snippets.

| Parameter | Type | Description |
|-----------|------|-------------|
| `config` | `QueryConfig` | Pipeline configuration (see below) |

**Returns:** `{ query(input: QueryInput) => Promise<QueryOutput> }`

```typescript

const agent = createQueryAgent({
  generateText,
  generateObject,
  documentStore,
  memoryStore,
  sourceRetriever,
  concurrency: 3,
  maxVerifyRounds: 1,
  retrievalLimit: 10,
  retrievalMode: "hybrid",
});
```

---

### `agent.query(input)`

Run the full query pipeline: interpret attachments, classify, plan retrieval, retrieve if needed, reason, verify, respond.

| Parameter | Type | Description |
|-----------|------|-------------|
| `input.question` | `string` | The user's question |
| `input.conversationId` | `string?` | Conversation ID for history context |
| `input.context` | `AgentContext?` | Platform and user context |
| `input.attachments` | `QueryAttachment[]?` | Optional inbound files or text context to interpret and combine with stored evidence |
| `input.retrievalMode` | `QueryRetrievalMode?` | Optional per-query source retrieval strategy hint |

**Returns:** `Promise<QueryOutput>`

```typescript
const result = await agent.query({
  question: "What is the deductible on our GL policy?",
  conversationId: "conv-123",
});

console.log(result.answer);      // Natural language answer with [1], [2] citations
console.log(result.citations);   // Citation[] with source references
console.log(result.confidence);  // 0-1 confidence score
console.log(result.intent);      // "policy_question"
console.log(result.followUp);    // Optional follow-up suggestion
console.log(result.tokenUsage);  // { inputTokens, outputTokens }
console.log(result.reviewReport); // Deterministic grounding/citation quality report
```

Retrieval is bounded by the classifier and planner. Simple general questions and attachment-only questions can skip document/chunk lookup entirely, while document-backed questions still retrieve evidence and verify grounding before responding.

### Multimodal example

```typescript
const result = await agent.query({
  question: "What else do we need to know about this, and does it match the stored policy?",
  conversationId: "conv-123",
  attachments: [
    {
      kind: "image",
      name: "wall-damage.jpg",
      mimeType: "image/jpeg",
      base64: damagePhotoBase64,
      description: "Photo the tenant texted in",
    },
    {
      kind: "pdf",
      name: "coi.pdf",
      mimeType: "application/pdf",
      base64: coiPdfBase64,
    },
    {
      kind: "text",
      name: "email-context",
      text: "Carrier asked whether the certificate holder needs waiver of subrogation.",
    },
  ],
});
```

---

## Types

### `QueryConfig`

```typescript
interface QueryConfig {
  generateText: GenerateText;
  generateObject: GenerateObject;
  documentStore: DocumentStore;
  memoryStore: MemoryStore;
  sourceRetriever?: SourceRetriever;
  concurrency?: number;           // default: 3
  maxVerifyRounds?: number;       // default: 1
  retrievalLimit?: number;        // default: 10
  retrievalMode?: QueryRetrievalMode;
  onTokenUsage?: (usage: TokenUsage) => void;
  onProgress?: (message: string) => void;
  log?: LogFn;
  providerOptions?: Record<string, unknown>;
  qualityGate?: "off" | "warn" | "strict";
  modelCapabilities?: ModelCapabilities;
  modelBudgetConstraints?: Partial<Record<ModelTaskKind, ModelBudgetConstraint>>;
}
```

### `QueryInput`

```typescript
interface QueryInput {
  question: string;
  conversationId?: string;
  context?: AgentContext;
  attachments?: QueryAttachment[];
  retrievalMode?: QueryRetrievalMode;
}
```

### `QueryRetrievalMode`

```typescript
type QueryRetrievalMode =
  | "graph_only"
  | "source_rag"
  | "long_context"
  | "hybrid";
```

### `QueryAttachment`

```typescript
interface QueryAttachment {
  id?: string;
  kind: "image" | "pdf" | "text";
  name?: string;
  mimeType?: string;
  base64?: string;      // image/pdf content
  text?: string;        // text attachment content
  description?: string; // optional caller hint
}
```

### `QueryOutput`

```typescript
interface QueryOutput {
  answer: string;
  citations: Citation[];
  intent: QueryIntent;
  confidence: number;
  followUp?: string;
  tokenUsage: TokenUsage;
  reviewReport: QueryReviewReport;
}
```

### `Citation`

```typescript
interface Citation {
  index: number;
  chunkId: string;
  documentId: string;
  documentType?: "policy" | "quote";
  field?: string;
  quote: string;
  relevance: number;
}
```

### `QueryIntent`

```typescript
type QueryIntent =
  | "policy_question"
  | "coverage_comparison"
  | "document_search"
  | "claims_inquiry"
  | "general_knowledge";
```

---

## Query prompt functions

These are exported for advanced use (custom pipelines, testing):

### `buildQueryClassifyPrompt(question, conversationContext?, attachmentContext?)`

Build the classification prompt for intent detection and sub-question decomposition.

### `buildInterpretAttachmentPrompt(question, attachment)`

Build the attachment interpretation prompt used for inbound image/PDF/text context.

### `buildReasonPrompt(subQuestion, intent, evidence)`

Build an intent-specific reasoning prompt with evidence context.

### `buildVerifyPrompt(originalQuestion, subAnswersJson, evidenceJson)`

Build the verification prompt for grounding, consistency, and completeness checks.

### `buildRespondPrompt(originalQuestion, subAnswersJson, platform?)`

Build the final response formatting prompt with platform-specific formatting guidance.

## Provider callback requirements for attachments

The query agent can pass multimodal payloads through `providerOptions` when attachments are present:

- `providerOptions.attachments` for the raw attachment list
- `providerOptions.pdfBase64` for PDF attachments
- `providerOptions.images` for image attachments
Your `generateObject` callback must translate those fields into actual provider-specific file or image parts. If it does not, attachment-aware queries will silently degrade into text-only behavior.

## Builders

```typescript
  buildSourceSpan,
  buildPageSourceSpans,
  buildSectionSourceSpans,
  buildTextSourceSpans,
  chunkSourceSpans,
} from "@claritylabs/cl-sdk";
```

- `buildSourceSpan(input, localIndex?)`: build one normalized `SourceSpan`.
- `buildPageSourceSpans(pages)`: build one span per page.
- `buildSectionSourceSpans(pages, options?)`: split pages into section candidate spans.
- `buildTextSourceSpans(input, options?)`: split long text into stable spans.
- `chunkSourceSpans(spans, options?)`: group spans into `SourceChunk[]`.

## Store and retriever

```typescript

const store = new MemorySourceStore();
await store.addSourceSpans(spans);
await store.addSourceChunks(chunks);
const results = await store.searchSourceSpans({ question, limit: 5 });
```

Implement `SourceStore` for production persistence:

```typescript
interface SourceStore extends SourceRetriever {
  addSourceSpans(spans: SourceSpan[]): Promise<void>;
  addSourceChunks(chunks: SourceChunk[]): Promise<void>;
  getSourceSpan(id: string): Promise<SourceSpan | null>;
  getSourceSpansByDocument(documentId: string): Promise<SourceSpan[]>;
  getSourceChunksByDocument(documentId: string): Promise<SourceChunk[]>;
  deleteDocumentSource(documentId: string): Promise<void>;
}
```

## Ordering helpers

`orderSourceEvidence()` sorts evidence by descending relevance and stable tie-break ID. Use it when combining vector, lexical, and structured retrieval results.

CL SDK provides PDF page utilities plus two modes for writing data onto PDFs using `pdf-lib`: **AcroForm filling** for fillable PDFs and **text overlay** for flat/scanned PDFs.

## Page utilities

Extract page ranges or get page counts without full parsing — useful for reducing token usage.

```typescript

// Get page count
const totalPages = await getPdfPageCount(pdfBase64);

// Extract only the declarations pages (pages 1-5) for classification
const declsOnly = await extractPageRange(pdfBase64, 1, 5);

// Returns the original if the range covers all pages
const same = await extractPageRange(pdfBase64, 1, totalPages);
```

## AcroForm fields

### Detect fields

Enumerate all fillable form fields in a PDF:

```typescript

const pdfDoc = await PDFDocument.load(pdfBytes);
const fields = getAcroFormFields(pdfDoc);

// [
//   { name: "insured_name", type: "text" },
//   { name: "agree_terms", type: "checkbox" },
//   { name: "state", type: "dropdown", options: ["CA", "NY", "TX"] },
// ]
```

Returns an empty array if the PDF has no AcroForm.

**Field types:** `text`, `checkbox`, `dropdown`, `radio`

### Fill and flatten

Fill AcroForm fields by name and flatten the form (makes fields non-editable):

```typescript

const mappings: FieldMapping[] = [
  { acroFormName: "insured_name", value: "Acme Corp" },
  { acroFormName: "effective_date", value: "01/01/2025" },
  { acroFormName: "agree_terms", value: "yes" },  // checks the checkbox
  { acroFormName: "state", value: "CA" },          // selects dropdown option
];

const filledPdfBytes = await fillAcroForm(pdfBytes, mappings);
```

**Checkbox values:** `"yes"`, `"true"`, `"x"`, `"checked"`, `"on"` check the box. Anything else unchecks it.

Fields not found in the PDF or values not in dropdown/radio options are silently skipped.

## Text overlay

For flat or scanned PDFs without form fields, overlay text at specific coordinates:

```typescript

const overlays: TextOverlay[] = [
  {
    page: 0,          // 0-indexed page number
    x: 25.5,          // percentage from left edge (0-100)
    y: 15.2,          // percentage from top edge (0-100)
    text: "Acme Corp",
    fontSize: 10,
  },
  {
    page: 0,
    x: 60,
    y: 30,
    text: "",
    isCheckmark: true,  // Draws an "X" instead of text
    fontSize: 12,
  },
];

const overlaidPdfBytes = await overlayTextOnPdf(pdfBytes, overlays);
```

### Coordinate system

- **`x`** — percentage from the left edge (0 = left, 100 = right)
- **`y`** — percentage from the top edge (0 = top, 100 = bottom)
- Coordinates are converted internally to pdf-lib's bottom-left origin system

### TextOverlay type

```typescript
interface TextOverlay {
  page: number;       // 0-indexed page number
  x: number;          // percentage from left (0-100)
  y: number;          // percentage from top (0-100)
  text: string;
  fontSize?: number;  // default: 10
  isCheckmark?: boolean;
}
```

Overlays for pages that don't exist are silently skipped.

The extraction pipeline returns a source-native `ExtractionResult`: a canonical source tree, the source spans used for grounding, a source-backed operational profile, and a compatibility `InsuranceDocument` projection. On the current v3 source-tree path, `chunks` is empty; hosts should index `sourceTree` and keep `sourceSpans` for exact highlights.

## ExtractionResult

```typescript
interface ExtractionResult {
  document: InsuranceDocument;  // Compatibility policy or quote projection
  chunks: DocumentChunk[];       // Empty on v3 source-tree extraction paths
  sourceSpans: SourceSpan[];     // Source evidence spans, including page/row/cell hierarchy when available
  sourceChunks: SourceChunk[];   // Retrieval windows built from source spans
  sourceTree?: DocumentSourceNode[]; // Canonical source hierarchy
  operationalProfile?: PolicyOperationalProfile; // Source-backed product facts
  warnings?: string[];
  tokenUsage: TokenUsage;        // Cumulative { inputTokens, outputTokens }
  usageReporting: {
    modelCalls: number;
    callsWithUsage: number;
    callsMissingUsage: number;
  };
  performanceReport: PerformanceReport;
  reviewReport: ExtractionReviewReport;
}
```

```typescript
const { document, sourceTree, operationalProfile, tokenUsage, usageReporting } =
  await extractor.extract(pdfBase64, "doc-123", { sourceSpans });
```

## InsuranceDocument

`InsuranceDocument` is a discriminated union of `PolicyDocument` and `QuoteDocument`, distinguished by the `type` field:

```typescript
type InsuranceDocument = PolicyDocument | QuoteDocument;
```

Both types share a large set of base fields and diverge on type-specific fields.

### Shared fields

These fields appear on both `PolicyDocument` and `QuoteDocument`:

| Field | Type | Description |
|-------|------|-------------|
| `id` | `string` | Document ID |
| `type` | `"policy" \| "quote"` | Discriminator |
