### Type narrowing

Use the `type` discriminator to narrow the union:

```typescript
const { document } = await extractor.extract(pdfBase64, "doc-123", { sourceSpans });

if (document.type === "policy") {
  console.log(document.policyNumber);     // string
  console.log(document.effectiveDate);    // string
} else {
  console.log(document.quoteNumber);      // string
  console.log(document.subjectivities);   // Subjectivity[] | undefined
}
```

## DocumentChunk

`DocumentChunk` is the legacy structured-document retrieval unit produced by `chunkDocument()`. Current v3 source-tree extraction returns `chunks: []`; use `sourceTree` and `sourceSpans` for new extraction retrieval.

```typescript
interface DocumentChunk {
  /** Deterministic ID: `${documentId}:${type}:${index}` */
  id: string;
  /** Source document ID */
  documentId: string;
  /** Chunk type for filtering */
  type: "carrier_info" | "named_insured" | "coverage"
      | "definition" | "covered_reason" | "endorsement"
      | "exclusion" | "condition" | "section" | "declaration"
      | "loss_history" | "premium" | "supplementary"
      | "location" | "vehicle" | "classification" | "financial"
      | "party" | "subjectivity" | "underwriting_condition";
  /** Human-readable text for embedding */
  text: string;
  /** Structured metadata for filtering */
  metadata: Record<string, string>;
}
```

### Chunk types

| Type | Source | Content |
|------|--------|---------|
| `carrier_info` | Carrier extractor | Carrier name, NAIC, AM Best, MGA, underwriter, broker |
| `named_insured` | Named insured extractor | Insured name, DBA, address, entity type |
| `coverage` | Coverage limits extractor | One chunk per coverage with limits and deductibles |
| `definition` | Definitions extractor | One chunk per defined term |
| `covered_reason` | Covered reasons extractor | One chunk per covered cause/reason plus one chunk per attached condition |
| `endorsement` | Endorsements extractor | One chunk per endorsement with form number and content |
| `exclusion` | Exclusions extractor | One chunk per exclusion |
| `condition` | Conditions extractor | One chunk per policy condition |
| `section` | Sections extractor | Raw section content |
| `declaration` | Declarations extractor | Line-specific declaration data |
| `loss_history` | Loss history extractor | Loss runs and claim records |
| `premium` | Premium breakdown extractor | Premium, taxes, fees, payment plans |
| `supplementary` | Supplementary extractor | Regulatory context, TPA, contacts, and hidden retrieval-only facts |

### Hidden supplementary facts

The extraction pipeline runs a second-pass `supplementary` extraction across the full document after the page-mapped strict extractors finish. The goal is to preserve useful facts that do not belong in the primary visible schema but are still valuable for memory and retrieval.

Those facts are stored on the document as `supplementaryFacts` and are also embedded into the `supplementary` chunk type for vector search.

Typical examples include:

- `policyholder_name`
- `insured_name`
- `driver_age`
- `driver_date_of_birth`
- `garaging_zip`
- `vehicle_principal_driver`

Each fact is a structured record:

```typescript
{
  key: string;
  value: string;
  subject?: string;
  context?: string;
}
```

Use `subject` to associate the fact with a specific person, vehicle, or scheduled item, and `context` to keep light provenance such as `"Driver Schedule"` or `"Named Insured"`.

<Callout type="info">
`supplementaryFacts` are intended for hidden memory and retrieval workflows. The SDK stores them on the returned document for persistence, but consumer applications typically should not render them by default.
</Callout>

### Deterministic chunk IDs

Chunk IDs follow the pattern `${documentId}:${type}:${index}`, where `index` is a zero-based counter within each type. This makes chunk IDs deterministic and idempotent — re-extracting the same document produces the same chunk IDs, enabling safe upserts.

```typescript
// Example chunk IDs for document "doc-123":
// "doc-123:carrier_info:0"
// "doc-123:coverage:0"
// "doc-123:coverage:1"
// "doc-123:coverage:2"
// "doc-123:endorsement:0"
// "doc-123:endorsement:1"
```

## Persisting results

The `ExtractionResult` is designed to feed directly into the SDK's storage interfaces:

```typescript

const extractor = createExtractor({
  generateObject: myGenerateObject,
});

const { document, sourceTree, sourceSpans: resultSourceSpans, tokenUsage, usageReporting } =
  await extractor.extract(pdfBase64, "doc-123", { sourceSpans });

// Save structured document to DocumentStore
await documentStore.save(document);

// Save sourceTree/sourceSpans to your source evidence index for retrieval and highlights
await sourceIndex.save({ sourceTree, sourceSpans: resultSourceSpans });
```

<Callout type="info">
`DocumentStore` and `MemoryStore` are interfaces defined by the SDK. The SDK includes a reference SQLite implementation, but you can implement these interfaces with any storage backend.
</Callout>

## Token usage

The `tokenUsage` field on `ExtractionResult` provides cumulative input and output token counts across all model calls that actually return usage metadata.

```typescript
interface TokenUsage {
  inputTokens: number;
  outputTokens: number;
}
```

```typescript
const { tokenUsage, usageReporting } = await extractor.extract(pdfBase64, "doc-123", { sourceSpans });
console.log(`Cost: ${tokenUsage.inputTokens} input + ${tokenUsage.outputTokens} output tokens`);
console.log(`Usage reported for ${usageReporting.callsWithUsage}/${usageReporting.modelCalls} model calls`);
```

For per-call tracking, use the `onTokenUsage` callback in `ExtractorConfig`. If some provider calls omit usage, `usageReporting.callsMissingUsage` will make that visible.

## Compatibility projection

The source-tree pipeline projects source-backed facts into the legacy `InsuranceDocument` shape so existing hosts can keep rendering familiar fields:

- **Identity fields** — `insuredName`, `carrier`, `policyNumber`, broker, and producer values come from `operationalProfile` when available. Persist source-backed named-insured facts from `operationalProfile.declarationFacts`, including mailing address, DBA, entity type, tax ID, and additional named insured rows, when hosts need durable org or client profiles.
- **Coverage fields** — compatibility `document.coverages` is projected from `operationalProfile.coverages`, but product surfaces should prefer the operational profile because it preserves nested terms and source IDs.
- **Document structure** — `documentMetadata` and `documentOutline` are materialized from `sourceTree`.

Financial fields such as premium, total cost, taxes, fees, payment plan, limits, deductibles, declaration facts, and source-backed coverage terms should come from `operationalProfile` or another cited source-backed record. Source-backed coverage terms also keep item references textual: `shown in Item 7` is provenance text, not `amount: 7`.

The compatibility projection should not be treated as a new source of policy wording. It is a view over source-backed facts.

## Null handling

The assembler uses `sanitizeNulls` internally to convert `null` values to `undefined` throughout the document. This ensures compatibility with frameworks like Convex that reject `null` for optional fields. You do not need to call `sanitizeNulls` yourself on extraction results.

# Form Structure in Source-Tree Extraction

The current extraction path does not run a separate form-inventory model pass or page-map model pass. Form structure is represented in the source tree and projected into compatibility fields where needed.

## Source-tree structure

Parser-provided spans are organized into `DocumentSourceNode` records:

```typescript
type DocumentSourceNodeKind =
  | "document"
  | "page_group"
  | "page"
  | "form"
  | "endorsement"
  | "section"
  | "schedule"
  | "clause"
  | "table"
  | "table_row"
  | "table_cell"
  | "text";
```

The grouping code uses source text, parser titles, form numbers, and page structure to identify declarations, policy forms, endorsements, sections, and schedules. It may reorganize existing nodes, but it must keep every node tied to real source spans.

## Form inventory compatibility

`document.formInventory`, `document.documentMetadata.formInventory`, and `reviewReport.formInventory` are compatibility projections from source-tree form nodes. They are useful for existing UIs, but they are not the canonical source structure. The canonical structure is `result.sourceTree`.

```typescript
interface FormReference {
  formNumber: string;
  editionDate?: string;
  title?: string;
  formType: "coverage" | "endorsement" | "declarations" | "application" | "notice" | "other";
}
```

## Coverage provenance

For v3 source-backed extraction, prefer `operationalProfile.coverages` over legacy flat compatibility rows. Each coverage is a source-backed unit and can contain nested `limits[]` terms:

```typescript
interface OperationalCoverageLine {
  name: string;
  coverageCode?: string;
  limit?: string;
  deductible?: string;
  premium?: string;
  retroactiveDate?: string;
  formNumber?: string;
  sectionRef?: string;
  endorsementNumber?: string;
  limits: OperationalCoverageTerm[];
  sourceNodeIds: string[];
  sourceSpanIds: string[];
}
```

Declaration rows such as `Item 7. Limits of Liability` can preserve nested each-claim, aggregate, retention, deductible, premium, and retroactive-date terms. Endorsement schedules should remain whole endorsement coverage units with adjacent schedule labels paired to their value cells.

Item references remain textual provenance terms. For example, an endorsement value such as `shown in Item 7` is retained as text and does not receive a fabricated numeric `amount: 7`.

## Practical guidance

- Index `result.sourceTree` for document navigation and retrieval.
- Use `sourceSpanIds` and parser bounding boxes for PDF highlights.
- Keep source-heading-derived titles terse.
- Do not maintain a second form-grouping system in the host app when the SDK source tree already groups the document.
- Do not use old page-map extractor assignments to decide whether a section is a coverage, endorsement, condition, or exclusion.

CL SDK 3.2 uses a source-tree extraction pipeline. The host owns PDF parsing; CL SDK owns deterministic source-tree construction, source-text grouping, source-backed operational fact extraction, citation validation, coverage cleanup, and compatibility projection.

Raw-PDF page-map extraction, focused extractor dispatch, form-inventory model calls, source-tree organizer model calls, and review-mode follow-up extractors are not part of the current published extraction path.

## Quick start

```typescript

const sourceSpans = buildPageSourceSpans([
  {
    documentId: "doc-123",
    sourceKind: "policy_pdf",
    pageNumber: 1,
    text: pageOneText,
  },
]);

const extractor = createExtractor({
  generateObject: myGenerateObject,
  onProgress: (msg) => console.log(msg),
});

const result = await extractor.extract(pdfBase64, "doc-123", { sourceSpans });

console.log(result.sourceTree?.length);          // canonical source hierarchy
console.log(result.operationalProfile?.coverages); // source-backed coverage units
console.log(result.document);                    // compatibility InsuranceDocument projection
console.log(result.chunks.length);               // 0 on v3 source-tree paths
```

<Callout type="info">
  PDF inputs require `ExtractOptions.sourceSpans`. Use LiteParse, Docling, PDF.js, OCR, or another parser before calling the SDK. Docling inputs are the exception because the SDK derives spans from the serialized Docling document.
</Callout>

## Pipeline overview

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A[Parser Source Spans] --> B[Normalize + Merge Spans]
    B --> C[Build Deterministic Source Tree]
    C --> D[Group Forms, Endorsements, Sections, Schedules]
    D --> E[Operational Profile Model Pass]
    E --> F[Validate Source IDs + Merge]
    F --> G{Coverages present?}
    G -->|yes| H[Coverage Cleanup Model Pass]
    G -->|no| I[Compatibility Projection]
    H --> I
    I --> J[SourceTree + OperationalProfile + Document]
```

## Phase 1: Normalize source spans

The coordinator merges spans derived from a Docling input with spans passed in `ExtractOptions.sourceSpans`. It normalizes text, removes unusable repeated boilerplate, preserves original span IDs in metadata when text is merged, and derives compatibility `SourceChunk[]` windows.

If no source spans remain, extraction throws. This is intentional: policy extraction no longer falls back to raw PDF page maps.

## Phase 2: Persist source evidence

When `sourceStore` is configured, the coordinator stores source spans and source chunks before building the tree:

```typescript
const extractor = createExtractor({
  generateObject,
  sourceStore,
});
```

Production hosts should also persist `result.sourceTree` in their own hierarchy/index tables. `SourceStore` persists spans and chunks; source-tree persistence is host-owned.

## Phase 3: Build the source tree

`buildDocumentSourceTree()` converts `SourceSpan[]` into `DocumentSourceNode[]`.

The tree preserves:

- document, page group, page, form, endorsement, section, schedule, clause, table, row, cell, and text node kinds
- `sourceSpanIds` for every node
- page ranges and bounding boxes when supplied by the parser
- source order
- stable hierarchy paths

Table structure matters. A Docling or parser row span is canonical evidence for table-derived facts; cell spans remain available for precise highlighting.

## Phase 4: Group source structure

The source-tree extractor groups existing nodes into declarations, policy forms, endorsements, sections, and schedules using source text, form numbers, parser titles, and page structure.

Important constraints:

- grouping may relabel or move existing nodes
- grouping must not invent node IDs, text, page numbers, source spans, or bounding boxes
- separately numbered endorsements remain separate endorsement nodes
- source-heading-derived titles stay terse
- generic table rows are retained in the tree but filtered out of operational-profile prompt context unless they carry product-relevant signals

## Phase 5: Extract the operational profile

The operational profile is the product fact projection. One `generateObject` call receives a bounded evidence packet of high-value declaration, schedule, premium, producer, endorsement, and coverage evidence.

It extracts:

- document type and `policyTypes`
- policy number
- named insured
- insurer and broker
- effective, expiration, and retroactive dates
- premium
- coverage units and nested `limits[]` terms
- parties
- endorsement support facts

Every fact must cite existing `sourceNodeIds` or `sourceSpanIds`. Uncited facts and unknown source IDs are rejected during merge.

## Phase 6: Clean up coverage units

When the profile contains coverages, the SDK may run one bounded cleanup pass. The cleanup pass can keep, drop, or update existing coverage rows and terms by original `coverageIndex`; it should repair projection shape, not invent unsupported coverage rows.

Endorsement schedules are modeled as whole endorsement coverage units with their own `limits[]` terms. The extractor should not flatten adjacent labels into unrelated rows like `Aggregate Limit` without endorsement context.

## Phase 7: Project compatibility output

The final result includes both source-native and compatibility views:

```typescript
interface ExtractionResult {
  sourceTree?: DocumentSourceNode[];
  operationalProfile?: PolicyOperationalProfile;
  sourceSpans: SourceSpan[];
  sourceChunks: SourceChunk[];
  document: InsuranceDocument;
  chunks: DocumentChunk[];
  warnings?: string[];
  tokenUsage: TokenUsage;
  usageReporting: {
    modelCalls: number;
    callsWithUsage: number;
    callsMissingUsage: number;
  };
  performanceReport: PerformanceReport;
  reviewReport: ExtractionReviewReport;
}
```

`document`, `documentMetadata`, and `documentOutline` are materialized views over `sourceTree` and `operationalProfile`. Keep existing screens working with `document`, but use `sourceTree` and `sourceSpans` for source viewers, retrieval, and citations. Host systems that persist named-insured profile facts should read source-backed mailing address, DBA, entity type, tax ID, and additional named insured rows from `operationalProfile.declarationFacts`.

`chunks` is empty on v3 source-tree extraction paths. Use `sourceTree` node descriptions and exact spans as the retrieval corpus.

## Provider callbacks

Extraction uses `GenerateObject`:

```typescript
type GenerateObject<T = unknown> = (params: {
  prompt: string;
  system?: string;
  schema: ZodSchema<T>;
  maxTokens: number;
  taskKind?: ModelTaskKind;
  budgetDiagnostics?: ModelBudgetResolution;
  providerOptions?: Record<string, unknown>;
  trace?: Record<string, unknown>;
}) => Promise<{ object: T; usage?: TokenUsage }>;
```

The callback should preserve `providerOptions.sourceSpans`, `providerOptions.sourceChunks`, and `trace` in host telemetry. The operational profile evidence is included in the prompt; source spans/chunks in provider options let hosts correlate model calls with parser evidence.

## Model routing and budgets

Hosts that route tasks to different models should pass per-task capabilities:

```typescript
const extractor = createExtractor({
  generateObject,
  modelCapabilitiesByTaskKind: {
    extraction_operational_profile: { maxOutputTokens: 16000 },
    extraction_coverage_cleanup: { maxOutputTokens: 8000 },
  },
});
```

`resolveModelBudget()` treats task budgets as preferences and diagnostics. When model capabilities provide `maxOutputTokens`, that model maximum is the request cap unless an explicit hard constraint lowers it. This avoids truncating extraction just because a cheap task preference was too low.

## Progress callbacks

Current extraction progress is intentionally small:

```typescript
const extractor = createExtractor({
  generateObject,
  onProgress: (message) => console.log(message),
});
```

Typical messages:

```text
Building source-native document tree...
Source-tree extraction complete.
```

## Retry and schema compatibility

The pipeline uses strictified Zod schemas before `generateObject` calls. `safeGenerateObject()` remains available for custom workflows that need the same strictification, retry, and fallback behavior.

CL SDK uses plain callback functions instead of framework-specific model objects. You provide functions that call your preferred LLM provider, and the SDK handles orchestration, retries, and concurrency. No framework dependency required.

## Callback types

There are four callback types. Source-tree extraction requires `generateObject`; query, application, PCE, and agent workflows also use `generateText`:

```typescript
type GenerateText = (params: {
  prompt: string;
  system?: string;
  maxTokens: number;
  taskKind?: ModelTaskKind;
  budgetDiagnostics?: ModelBudgetResolution;
  providerOptions?: Record<string, unknown>;
}) => Promise<{ text: string; usage?: TokenUsage }>;

type GenerateObject<T = unknown> = (params: {
  prompt: string;
  system?: string;
  schema: ZodSchema<T>;
  maxTokens: number;
  taskKind?: ModelTaskKind;
  budgetDiagnostics?: ModelBudgetResolution;
  providerOptions?: Record<string, unknown>;
}) => Promise<{ object: T; usage?: TokenUsage }>;

// Required for MemoryStore-backed vector search
type EmbedText = (text: string) => Promise<number[]>;

// Optional utility callback for custom PDF/image workflows outside current source-tree extraction
type ConvertPdfToImagesFn = (
  pdfBase64: string,
  startPage: number,
  endPage: number,
) => Promise<Array<{ imageBase64: string; mimeType: string }>>;
```

For source-tree extraction and multimodal query calls, `providerOptions` carries evidence and attachments:

- `providerOptions.sourceSpans` carries source evidence relevant to the current extraction/application/query call
- `providerOptions.sourceChunks` carries grouped source evidence windows derived from spans
- `providerOptions.attachments` carries generic query attachments such as inbound photos, PDFs, and text snippets
- `providerOptions.pdfBase64` carries PDF attachments for multimodal query/application workflows when available
- `providerOptions.images` carries image attachments when available
- query attachment interpretation calls may set both `providerOptions.attachments` and the convenience fields above for the current attachment

Current policy extraction does not send raw PDF bytes, page-scoped PDFs, or rendered page images as its primary evidence. It builds a source tree from parser-provided spans and sends bounded source evidence to `generateObject`.

`taskKind` is a provider-agnostic subtask label for host-side model routing, fallback, and telemetry. `budgetDiagnostics` carries the resolved token budget, task kind, truncation-risk estimate, and warnings. Hosts can route cheap-first by task and escalate only on their own risk/error policy; CL SDK does not hardcode provider or model names.

